thread:
ana: Running the browser sampleapp in CI (headless tabs) and seeing a huge spike of 429s whenever the identity proxy rotates short-lived tokens. Happens only when multiple tabs are open and background prefetch + paginator are enabled. Anyone seen cross-tab prefetching cause quota bursts?

jordan: browser-specific? service worker / multiple tabs can duplicate work if they each prefetch. Which SDK and auth flow?

ana: using ts-sdk 0.29.1 in a webapp. Auth is short-lived tokens from our identity proxy (TTL=30s) that auto-refresh. PrefetchAhead=24, maxConcurrent=10. In CI we spin 6 tabs; when token rotates all tabs immediately fire prefetch and we quickly hit tenant/user 429s for ~20s.

lee: captured response headers: x-ratelimit-user-limit:80, x-ratelimit-user-remaining:0, x-ratelimit-reset:2751234700, x-client-session:browser:tab_4, x-user-id:anon_77

edge-bot: note: browsers create new sockets/handshakes when auth header changes; multiple tabs = many new sessions hitting per-user limits simultaneously.

jordan: a few mitigations: 1) coordinate prefetch across tabs (leader election via BroadcastChannel or localStorage locks) so only one tab warms pages; 2) pause prefetch on token refresh and resume after a jittered delay; 3) reuse the same ServiceWorker fetch handler / keep a stable client-id header that persists across token swaps.

ana: quick proof: I added BroadcastChannel-based leader election so only one tab does prefetch. also pause prefetch for 800ms after refresh and add random resume 100-600ms. that dropped 429 spikes by ~85% in my run.

lee: nice. sharing a minimal leader election snippet might help other teams.

jordan: example leader code (very small):

```js
const bc = new BroadcastChannel('sampleapp-prefetch');
let leader = false;

function tryBecomeLeader(){
  bc.postMessage({type: 'who'});
  const timeout = setTimeout(()=>{ leader = true; bc.postMessage({type:'leader'}) }, 100);
  bc.onmessage = (ev)=>{
    if(ev.data?.type === 'leader') { clearTimeout(timeout); leader = false }
  }
}

// only leader runs prefetch loop
if(leader) runPrefetch();
```

ana: also for single-tab apps, reusing the same ServiceWorker fetch handler and attaching a stable x-client-id header (persisted in IndexedDB) helps the server map requests to the same session across token swaps.

edge-bot: caveat: some browsers will still open a new TCP connection when the Authorization header changes; keeping a stable custom header isn't foolproof for socket reuse, but it helps server session attribution.

jordan: extra tips: - cancel in-flight prefetch requests on rotate if possible; - reduce prefetch window for browser environments (12-16) compared to server-side; - respect Retry-After and surface x-user-rate headers in client telemetry so you can dynamically throttle.

ana: implemented cancel-on-rotate + lower prefetch window + BroadcastChannel leader -> CI stable. I also added logging for x-ratelimit-user-remaining to make it obvious when we're close to tenant/user bucket exhaustion.

lee: can we add a short "Browser: multi-tab prefetch" recipe to the devdocs with these patterns? includes leader election sample, service worker note, and recommended defaults for prefetch window in browsers.

devdocs: yes — will draft a doc: "Browser onboarding: avoid multi-tab prefetch storms" and include the code snippet + checklist: reuse SW, leader election, pause on auth rotate, cancel inflight, jittered resume, reduce prefetch window. I'll open PR #5012 and link it here.

jordan: also consider exposing a helper in the ts-sdk: PrefetchCoordinator that implements BroadcastChannel leader election + pauseOnAuthRotate hook so app authors don’t reimplement. filing an SDK task.

ana: thanks all ":thumbsup:" — will attach a small repro harness (6 headless tabs rotating tokens) to the ticket so infra can correlate with server traces. happy to help run it against staging.
